til a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes (cause `evicted`, a transient one; `Status.Transient` and `closeout.CauseEvicted` arrive in later parts of #54). The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\n174:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n202:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n203:3. **Scan.** Run the scanner on a read-only worktree of `origin/main`, with a timeout. It needs a free job slot, and scanners rank last for one. **A run that is denied a slot is not dropped: it is not recorded, stays due and is offered again on the next tick** (every five minutes), until it gets a slot or the controller stops. A failed scan (non-zero exit, timeout, invalid output) is recorded and changes nothing else.\n212:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\n238:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n240:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n246:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n260:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the